% An attendant control (E2331) in place of the beneficiary's interface, for
% a beneficiary who cannot operate any wheelchair and whose caregiver can
% operate a power one (criterion K of the base); a safety belt (E0978) for
% upper body instability; and adjustable armrests (claim 4) for a beneficiary
% who spends one hour a day in the chair.
scenario attendant_control_and_safety_belt is:
    claim 1 is for the attendant control.
    the HCPCS code of the attendant control is "E2331".
    the attendant control is an accessory of the power wheelchair.
    the attendant control is furnished to Gus.
    the attendant control is provided in place of a beneficiary operated drive control interface.
    the finding attendant control required about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "the beneficiary cannot operate a manual or power wheelchair; the spouse cannot propel a manual wheelchair but can operate a power wheelchair".
    claim 3 is for the safety belt.
    the HCPCS code of the safety belt is "E0978".
    the safety belt is an accessory of the power wheelchair.
    the safety belt is furnished to Gus.
    the finding safety belt needed for positioning about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "upper body instability".
    claim 4 is for the armrests.
    the HCPCS code of the armrests is "K0020".
    the armrests is an accessory of the power wheelchair.
    the armrests is furnished to Gus.
    the finding adjustable arm height needed about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the hours per day Gus spends in the wheelchair is 1.
    claim 2 is for the power wheelchair.
    the HCPCS code of the power wheelchair is "K0823".
    the power wheelchair is furnished to Gus.
    the rental month of claim 2 is 1.
    the finding significant mobility limitation about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding walking aids insufficient about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding manual wheelchair not self propellable about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding scooter ruled out about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding power wheelchair can be safely operated about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "a caregiver is available, willing and able to provide assistance with the power wheelchair".
    the finding home accommodates power wheelchair about Gus is established, according to the supplier.
    the finding power wheelchair will significantly improve daily activities about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding willing to use power wheelchair at home about Gus is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the weight of Gus is 200 pounds.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 3 is reasonable and necessary"].

% A Group 3 chair with a sip and puff interface (E2325, claim 1), the
% expandable controller it needs (E2377, claim 3), a power tilt system
% (E1002, claim 4), the multi-motor connection that drives it from the
% interface (E2310, claim 5), a speech generating device interface (E2351,
% claim 6), a power standing system (E2301, claim 7), which is non-covered,
% and swingaway hardware (E1028, claim 8) for a slide transfer.
scenario alternative_controls is:
    claim 1 is for the sip and puff interface.
    the HCPCS code of the sip and puff interface is "E2325".
    the sip and puff interface is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the sip and puff interface is furnished to Fay.
    the finding alternative drive control needed about Fay is established, according to the physical therapist, because "no functional use of the upper extremities for a hand or chin operated joystick".
    claim 3 is for the expandable controller.
    the HCPCS code of the expandable controller is "E2377".
    the expandable controller is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the expandable controller is furnished to Fay.
    claim 4 is for the tilt system.
    the HCPCS code of the tilt system is "E1002".
    the tilt system is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the tilt system is furnished to Fay.
    Fay had a specialty evaluation by a licensed medical professional with no financial relationship with the supplier for the tilt system.
    a RESNA certified assistive technology professional employed by the supplier was directly involved in the selection of the tilt system.
    the finding power seating system needed about Fay is established, according to the physical therapist, because "increased tone and spasticity".
    claim 5 is for the multi motor connection.
    the HCPCS code of the multi motor connection is "E2310".
    the multi motor connection is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the multi motor connection is furnished to Fay.
    claim 6 is for the speech device interface.
    the HCPCS code of the speech device interface is "E2351".
    the speech device interface is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the speech device interface is furnished to Fay.
    Fay has a covered speech generating device.
    claim 7 is for the standing system.
    the HCPCS code of the standing system is "E2301".
    the standing system is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the standing system is furnished to Fay.
    claim 8 is for the swingaway hardware.
    the HCPCS code of the swingaway hardware is "E1028".
    the swingaway hardware is an accessory of the rehab chair.
    the swingaway hardware is furnished to Fay.
    the finding swingaway hardware needed about Fay is established, according to the physical therapist, because "the head control must move out of the way for a slide transfer to the bed".
    claim 2 is for the rehab chair.
    the HCPCS code of the rehab chair is "K0856".
    the rehab chair is furnished to Fay.
    the rental month of claim 2 is 1.
    the finding significant mobility limitation about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding walking aids insufficient about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding manual wheelchair not self propellable about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding scooter ruled out about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding power wheelchair can be safely operated about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding home accommodates power wheelchair about Fay is established, according to the supplier.
    the finding power wheelchair will significantly improve daily activities about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding willing to use power wheelchair at home about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding qualifying group 3 diagnosis about Fay is established, according to the treating practitioner, because "amyotrophic lateral sclerosis".
    the finding single power option justified about Fay is established, according to the physical therapist.
    Fay had a specialty evaluation by a licensed medical professional with no financial relationship with the supplier for the rehab chair.
    a RESNA certified assistive technology professional employed by the supplier was directly involved in the selection of the rehab chair.
    the weight of Fay is 150 pounds.
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 3 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 4 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 5 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 6 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 8 is reasonable and necessary"].

% Reasonable and necessary, but the written report of the specialty
% evaluation is missing: the option is not payable, and that report is the
% one change that would make it so. The base has its order, face-to-face
% encounter and delivery (the WOPD the option's payment needs) but neither
% the home assessment nor the prior authorization, so it is not payable
% either. "stage" is not asked here: with two policies loaded, the guard of
% pmd.le's documentation rule ("the policy of the claim is pmd") is a goal
% that fails for the option, and its rules are in applicability, which the
% checklist then blames — see no_proof_of_delivery for the stage.
scenario no_written_report is:
    claim 1 is for the tilt system.
    the HCPCS code of the tilt system is "E1002".
    the tilt system is an accessory of the power wheelchair.
    the tilt system is furnished to Ann.
    the date of service of claim 1 is 2026-04-10.
    Ann had a specialty evaluation by a licensed medical professional with no financial relationship with the supplier for the tilt system.
    a RESNA certified assistive technology professional employed by the supplier was directly involved in the selection of the tilt system.
    the finding power seating system needed about Ann is established, according to the physical therapist, because "high risk for development of a pressure ulcer and unable to perform a functional weight shift".
    the order for the tilt system is dated 2026-02-15.
    the order for the tilt system is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the tilt system includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the tilt system includes order date.
    the order for the tilt system includes description of the item.
    the order for the tilt system includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the tilt system includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the tilt system was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-16.
    the tilt system was delivered on 2026-04-10.
    the supplier holds proof of delivery for the tilt system.
    claim 2 is for the power wheelchair.
    the HCPCS code of the power wheelchair is "K0823".
    the power wheelchair is furnished to Ann.
    the residence of Ann is private residence.
    the date of service of claim 2 is 2026-04-10.
    the rental month of claim 2 is 1.
    the finding significant mobility limitation about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding walking aids insufficient about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding manual wheelchair not self propellable about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding scooter ruled out about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding power wheelchair can be safely operated about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding home accommodates power wheelchair about Ann is established, according to the supplier.
    the finding power wheelchair will significantly improve daily activities about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the finding willing to use power wheelchair at home about Ann is established, according to the treating practitioner.
    the weight of Ann is 180 pounds.
    the treating practitioner examined Ann for the power wheelchair in a face-to-face encounter on 2026-02-01.
    the order for the power wheelchair is dated 2026-02-15.
    the order for the power wheelchair is signed by the treating practitioner.
    the order for the power wheelchair includes beneficiary name.
    the order for the power wheelchair includes order date.
    the order for the power wheelchair includes description of the item.
    the order for the power wheelchair includes quantity to be dispensed.
    the order for the power wheelchair includes treating practitioner name.
    the order for the power wheelchair was communicated to the supplier on 2026-02-16.
    the power wheelchair was delivered on 2026-04-10.
    pay expects answers [].
    rn expects answers ["claim 1 is reasonable and necessary", "claim 2 is reasonable and necessary"].
    flip expects changes [["add: the written report of the specialty evaluation for the tilt system is available"]].

